# API Proposal: JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396) for ASP.NET Core

Related: [#35237](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/35237)

## Motivation

Issue #35237 asks for built-in JSON Merge Patch support alongside JSON Patch. JSON Patch is available through `Microsoft.AspNetCore.JsonPatch.SystemTextJson`, but its operation-array format is a different protocol. A Merge Patch request describes changes using the shape of the resource itself:

```http
PATCH /products/42 HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json

{"name":"New name","address":{"city":"Paris"},"obsoleteField":null}
```

The original issue cites RFC 7386; [RFC 7396](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7396.html) obsoletes it. At the JSON-document level, a patch such as `{"a":null}` **removes the `a` member**. When applying the same patch directly to a C# model object, a declared property cannot disappear, so the typed API maps that removal to `null` or the property's default value. An omitted member means “leave it alone.” Deserializing the request directly into `Product` loses the distinction between omitted and explicit `null`.

## Current state and gap

- ASP.NET Core already has [System.Text.Json-based JSON Patch support](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/web-api/jsonpatch?view=aspnetcore-10.0). It implements RFC 6902 operations rather than RFC 7396 merge semantics.
- The MVC JSON input formatter [accepts `application/*+json`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/openapi/include-metadata?view=aspnetcore-10.0#describe-the-request-body), which covers `application/merge-patch+json`. That provides request-body deserialization, but no merge algorithm or representation that retains member presence.
- A normal C# model cannot represent a declared JSON property being absent. A JSON-level API is therefore needed for fully conforming output, even if a typed convenience API is also offered.

## Design goals

1. Implement RFC 7396 for **any JSON value**, including an object, array, scalar, or root-level `null` patch.
2. Preserve the distinction between an omitted member and a member whose patch value is `null`.
3. Work with MVC Controllers and Minimal APIs using the registered `application/merge-patch+json` media type.
4. Integrate with System.Text.Json metadata and allow callers to supply `JsonTypeInfo<T>`.
5. Limit the library to binding and applying the patch. The application's endpoint loads the target resource, checks the caller's permission and resource version (for example, an ETag), validates the updated model against business rules, and saves it.

## Proposed package and architecture

Add `Microsoft.AspNetCore.JsonMergePatch.SystemTextJson` under `src/Features/JsonMergePatch.SystemTextJson/`, following the package boundary used by `JsonPatch.SystemTextJson`. A representative layout is:

```text
src/Features/JsonMergePatch.SystemTextJson/
├── src/
│   ├── JsonMergePatchDocument.cs
│   ├── JsonMergePatchDocumentOfT.cs
│   ├── Adapters/
│   │   ├── ObjectAdapter.cs
│   │   └── AdapterFactory.cs
│   ├── Converters/
│   │   ├── JsonMergePatchDocumentConverter.cs
│   │   ├── JsonMergePatchDocumentConverterFactory.cs
│   │   └── JsonConverterForJsonMergePatchDocumentOfT.cs
│   ├── Internal/
│   │   ├── JsonMergePatchWriter.cs
│   │   ├── ObjectVisitor.cs
│   │   ├── PocoAdapter.cs
│   │   └── DictionaryAdapterOfT.cs
│   └── Microsoft.AspNetCore.JsonMergePatch.SystemTextJson.csproj
└── test/
    ├── JsonMergePatchDocumentTest.cs
    └── JsonMergePatchContentTypeEndToEndTest.cs
```

The patch document stores the JSON value as UTF-8 bytes, including explicit `null` values; the HTTP converter need not preserve the original whitespace or member order. The JSON API produces an RFC 7396 result, while the typed API applies member changes to a mutable C# object. A root-level JSON `null` is a valid patch value and must remain distinguishable from a missing request body.

## Proposed public API

This proposal uses the non-generic document for strict JSON-level behavior and `JsonMergePatchDocument<TModel>` for direct updates to a C# model, similar to the existing `JsonPatchDocument<TModel>` shape:

```csharp
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.JsonMergePatch.SystemTextJson;

public sealed class JsonMergePatchDocument : IEndpointParameterMetadataProvider
{
    public const string MediaType = "application/merge-patch+json";

    public ReadOnlyMemory<byte> Patch { get; }

    public JsonMergePatchDocument(ReadOnlySpan<byte> patchUtf8Json);

    // RFC 7396 result. An empty span means there is no target JSON value.
    public byte[] ApplyTo(ReadOnlySpan<byte> targetUtf8Json);

    public static void PopulateMetadata(ParameterInfo parameter, EndpointBuilder builder);
}

public sealed class JsonMergePatchDocument<TModel> : IEndpointParameterMetadataProvider
    where TModel : class
{
    public ReadOnlyMemory<byte> Patch { get; }

    public JsonMergePatchDocument(ReadOnlySpan<byte> patchUtf8Json);

    // Mutates a mutable target for an object patch. For a non-object root
    // patch, returns a replacement if it can be represented as TModel.
    public TModel? ApplyTo(TModel? target);
    public TModel? ApplyTo(TModel? target, JsonTypeInfo<TModel> typeInfo);

    public static void PopulateMetadata(ParameterInfo parameter, EndpointBuilder builder);
}
```

The documents would have internal System.Text.Json converters, so standard JSON request-body binding can construct them without a new DI registration. In Minimal APIs, `PopulateMetadata` adds `AcceptsMetadata` for `application/merge-patch+json`; [ASP.NET Core's routing policy](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Http/Routing/src/Matching/AcceptsMatcherPolicy.cs) uses that metadata to reject unsupported request content types with HTTP 415. MVC actions can use `[Consumes(JsonMergePatchDocument.MediaType)]` for the same restriction. The request-body schema shown in OpenAPI still needs to be checked separately because the patch can be any JSON value.

The typed API walks matching properties and string-keyed dictionary entries using System.Text.Json metadata. An object patch updates the existing object; a non-object root patch returns a replacement value. A `null` member sets a writable C# property to `null` or its type's default value, or removes a dictionary entry. Since a declared C# property cannot disappear, callers that need an absent JSON member should use the UTF-8 JSON API for the exact RFC 7396 result.

## Usage examples

### Exact JSON result

```csharp
var patch = new JsonMergePatchDocument("""{"email":null}"""u8);
byte[] result = patch.ApplyTo("""{"name":"Joe","email":"joe@example.com"}"""u8);
// result represents {"name":"Joe"}; the email member is absent.
```

### Controller

```csharp
[HttpPatch("{id:int}")]
[Consumes(JsonMergePatchDocument.MediaType)]
public ActionResult<Product> Patch(
    int id,
    [FromBody] JsonMergePatchDocument<Product> patch)
{
    var candidate = _products.Find(id);
    if (candidate is null)
    {
        return NotFound();
    }

    var updated = patch.ApplyTo(candidate);
    if (updated is null)
    {
        return BadRequest("This endpoint does not allow a null resource.");
    }

    // Run application-specific validation before saving the updated model.
    _products.Save(id, updated);
    return Ok(updated);
}
```

### Minimal API

```csharp
app.MapPatch("/products/{id:int}",
    (int id, JsonMergePatchDocument<Product> patch, IProductStore products) =>
    {
        var candidate = products.Find(id);
        if (candidate is null)
        {
            return Results.NotFound();
        }

        var updated = patch.ApplyTo(candidate);
        if (updated is null)
        {
            return Results.BadRequest();
        }

        products.Save(id, updated);
        return Results.Ok(updated);
    });
```

These examples use the one-parameter overload with web JSON defaults. Use `patch.ApplyTo(candidate, ProductJsonContext.Default.Product)` when the application needs a specific System.Text.Json contract or source-generated model metadata. Neither MVC nor Minimal API JSON settings are automatically passed to `ApplyTo(candidate)`. The one-parameter overload should not be presented as a Native AOT path; full AOT support also depends on how converters and adapters are created. `Find` represents application-specific resource lookup; the library does not prescribe whether it returns a tracked object or a copy. The examples omit application-specific authorization, validation, and concurrency checks, which must run before `Save` commits the result.

## Behavioral details

| Patch | Result at the JSON level |
| --- | --- |
| Object member omitted | Keep the target member unchanged. |
| Object member set to `null` | Remove that member, if present. |
| Object member set to an object | Recursively merge with the target member. |
| Object member set to an array | Replace the entire array. |
| Root patch is an array, scalar, or `null` | Replace the entire target value. |
| Target is not an object, patch is an object | Start from an empty object, then merge. |

These are JSON semantics. When a typed patch resets a C# property to its default value, serializing the object again may still emit that property. A non-object root patch that cannot be represented as `TModel` fails deserialization; the UTF-8 JSON API still supports it. RFC 7396 also leaves behavior for duplicate object member names undefined; tests should not imply a stronger guarantee.

For HTTP PATCH, [RFC 5789 requires atomic application](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789.html#section-2). `ApplyTo(TModel)` mutates the supplied object and does not guarantee rollback if a later member fails. The application is responsible for applying the patch to a detached update model or using a transaction, then saving only after the patch and application validation succeed. The API documentation and endpoint examples should make this responsibility clear.

## Validation and tests

- Run every example in [RFC 7396 Appendix A](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7396.html#appendix-A), including non-object roots and nested removals.
- Test JSON `null` request-body binding as a document while an empty body is rejected, object-member presence, dictionary/object nesting, array replacement, malformed JSON, depth limits, and unknown members.
- Test MVC `[FromBody]` with `[Consumes]`, Minimal API binding and metadata, and a request with an unsupported content type.
- Test both typed overloads, including `JsonTypeInfo<T>` from a System.Text.Json source-generated context, `[JsonPropertyName]`, case-insensitive matching, nested objects, dictionaries, a root replacement, and a C# property reset by a `null` patch member.
- Test failure after an earlier member update and document that the supplied model may be partially modified.
- If Native AOT support is intended, publish and exercise an AOT sample; using `JsonTypeInfo<T>` alone does not verify the converter and adapter creation paths.
- Document that authorization, allowed-field validation, and persistence remain application responsibilities, as described in [RFC 7396 security considerations](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7396.html#section-5).

## Questions for the team

1. For `ApplyTo(Product)`, what should happen if a patch contains a member that `Product` cannot represent, such as `{"nickname":"Ace"}` when `Product` has no `Nickname` property: reject it or ignore it? When applying the same patch to raw JSON, RFC 7396 adds the non-null `nickname` member.
2. Should typed `ApplyTo` report conversion and unwritable-property failures through an error callback, following [JSON Patch's `ApplyTo` pattern](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/web-api/jsonpatch?view=aspnetcore-10.0#apply-a-json-patch-document-to-an-object), or throw exceptions? The endpoint examples need a defined way to turn these failures into a client response.
3. How should OpenAPI describe a Merge Patch request body as an arbitrary JSON value rather than the document's `Patch` byte property? The media-type metadata alone does not settle the schema.
4. Should a document bound from an HTTP request remember that app's JSON options for `ApplyTo(candidate)`? The current proposal instead uses web defaults unless the caller supplies `JsonTypeInfo<TModel>`, which can produce different property-name and converter behavior from request binding.

I would be happy to refine the API and contribute an implementation and conformance tests once the desired scope is clear.

